8, "service_tier": "standard", "inference_geo": "global", "speed": "standard"}, "input_transformations": [], "diagnostics": null, "context_management": null}, "parent_tool_use_id": null, "timestamp": "2026-10-28T22:15:29.299Z", "request_id": "req_011CgVQWp9cSGy2q1D62Kkxh", "thinking_duration_ms": 35599}, {"type": "stream_event", "event": {"type": "content_block_stop", "index": 0}, "parent_tool_use_id": null}, {"type": "stream_event", "event": {"type": "content_block_start", "index": 1, "content_block": {"type": "text", "text": ""}}, "parent_tool_use_id": null}, {"type": "assistant", "message": {"model": "claude-opus-5-5", "id": "msg_011CgVQWpZfTCiz1DYZzBGLc", "type": "message", "role": "assistant", "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "The four I'd put in the top half are 3, 6, 0 and 5, but the last two are close calls. Everything below is mental arithmetic on the forecast values, with no simulation, so these are probabilities, not certainties.\n\n**What the plans share.** The opening stock of item 1 is 57 units, but forecast demand for days 0–4 is 208, and nothing ordered can arrive before day 5. So every plan starts with the same unavoidable shortage. The plans then differ in how often they order and how much they cover relative to the forecast.\n\n**Coverage ratio.** I divided each order by the forecast demand for the days its delivery covers. For example, item 1 ordered on day 0 arrives on day 5 and covers days 5–6, with forecast 82.\n\n| Plan | Item 0 coverage | Item 1 coverage | Ordering days |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| 2 | 0.91 | 0.9 | 13 days (every other day) |\n| 5 | 1.0 | 1.0 | 13 days |\n| 3 | 1.09 | 1.1 | 13 days |\n| 6 | 1.09 | 1.2 | 13 days |\n| 4 | 1.09 | 1.3 | 13 days |\n| 7 | 1.2 | 1.2 | 13 days |\n| 0 | about 1.1 | about 1.1 | 9 days (every third day) |\n| 1 | about 1.1 | about 1.1 | 27 days (daily) |\n\n**Where each plan loses money:**\n\n- **Item 1.** A missing unit costs 10 (7 of lost margin plus a 3 penalty). An extra unit costs 4 to buy plus 0.6 